  status WebFormSubmissionStatus [not null]
  reject_reason varchar(255) [default: '', not null]
  submitted_ip char(39)
  referer varchar(512) [default: '', not null]

  indexes {
    (created_by_id) [name: 'web_form_submission_created_by_id_52e13fb8']
    (updated_by_id) [name: 'web_form_submission_updated_by_id_e0bc1581']
    (org_id) [name: 'web_form_submission_org_id_7d2dccb5']
    (form_id) [name: 'web_form_submission_form_id_a78bd4f1']
    (lead_id) [name: 'web_form_submission_lead_id_17db4bc2']
    (case_id) [name: 'web_form_submission_case_id_857c590d']
    (org_id, created_at) [name: 'web_form_su_org_id_2c1d24_idx', note: 'descending: created_at']
    (form_id, created_at) [name: 'web_form_su_form_id_d12f93_idx', note: 'descending: created_at']
    (form_id, status) [name: 'web_form_su_form_id_890953_idx']
  }

  checks {
    `("lead_id" IS NULL OR "case_id" IS NULL)` [name: 'web_form_submission_lead_or_case']
  }

  Note: '''One POST from a visitor, accepted or not.

Rejected rows are kept. Without them there is no spam review, and the
conversion rate silently counts only the submissions that worked.

An accepted submission links the record it created: `lead` on a lead
form, `case` on a ticket form. Never both, which a check constraint
enforces. Neither is set on a rejected row, and either can become null
later because deleting the record keeps the submission.

Columns from BaseOrgModel (abstract): org_id.

Columns from BaseModel (abstract): id.

Columns from TimeAuditModel (abstract): created_at, updated_at.

Columns from UserAuditModel (abstract): created_by_id, updated_by_id.'''
}

